<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Gottlieb Fichte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Gottlieb Fichte was a German philosopher and a founding figure of German idealism, which developed from the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>. Born in Rammenau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, the son of a ribbon weaver, he first won attention with Attempt at a Critique of All Revelation (1792), published without his name and at first believed to be a new work by Kant. He taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> from 1794 until his dismissal for atheism in 1799, then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. His Addresses to the German Nation (1807–1808) gave him a reputation as one of the fathers of German nationalism. He was elected rector of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> and died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte.jpg" alt="Johann Gottlieb Fichte" width="330" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottlieb_Fichte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Immanuel Kant and the idealist philosophers who followed him, <b>Johann Gottlieb Fichte</b> and Friedrich Schelling, stressed the creative power of the mind.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
